CertHub.register({"id":"a-plus-core2","vendor":"CompTIA","name":"CompTIA A+ Core 2","short":"A+ Core 2","exam":"220-1202","blurb":"Entry-level IT support certification (second of two exams): Windows, macOS, Linux and mobile operating systems, endpoint security, software troubleshooting and operational procedures.","status":"check","statusNote":"Exam code 220-1202 (V15), weights 28/28/23/21, up to 90 questions in 90 minutes and a 700 passing score were confirmed on Sept 24, 2026 only from secondary sources that quote CompTIA's objectives; comptia.org and CompTIA's objectives PDF could not be opened from this environment, so re-check the weights on the official page.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[{"from":"2026-09-24","until":"2026-12-31","text":"The older 220-1102 exam retired Sept 25, 2025. This plan covers 220-1202 (V15), the current Core 2 exam, which launched March 25, 2025."}],"examInfo":{"questions":"Up to 90 (multiple choice and PBQs)","minutes":90,"pass":"700 on a 100–900 scale"},"examSim":{"questions":90,"minutes":90},"sources":[{"label":"CompTIA A+ Core 2 (220-1202) V15 exam page","url":"https://www.comptia.org/en-us/certifications/a/core-2-v15/"},{"label":"CompTIA A+ 220-1202 exam objectives (PDF)","url":"https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-a-220-1202-exam-objectives.pdf"}],"planWeeks":10,"hoursPerWeek":"6–8","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Operating systems","w":28,"topics":["OS types and purposes: Windows, macOS, Linux, ChromeOS, iOS/iPadOS, Android; vendor life cycles and end-of-life","File systems: NTFS, ReFS, FAT32, exFAT, ext4, XFS, APFS and their limits","Installations and upgrades: boot methods (USB, PXE, ISO), clean vs in-place vs image deployment vs repair install, GPT vs MBR, third-party drivers","Windows 10/11 editions (Home, Pro, Enterprise, Education) and which features each has: BitLocker, domain join, Group Policy, Remote Desktop host","Windows tools: Task Manager, MMC snap-ins (Event Viewer, Disk Management, Task Scheduler, Device Manager, Certificate Manager, Local Users and Groups, Performance Monitor, Group Policy Editor), msinfo32, Resource Monitor, System Configuration, Registry Editor, Disk Cleanup","Command-line tools: cd, dir, md, rmdir, robocopy, xcopy, diskpart, format, chkdsk, sfc, DISM, gpupdate, gpresult, net use, net user, whoami, winver, shutdown, ipconfig, ping, tracert, pathping, nslookup, netstat, hostname","Settings and Control Panel: Accounts, Privacy, Update and Security, Apps, Power Options (sleep, hibernate, fast startup), Display, Devices","Windows networking: workgroup vs domain, mapped drives and shares, firewall exceptions, static vs DHCP addressing, VPN and proxy settings, public vs private network profiles, metered connections","MacOS: installing and removing apps (.dmg, .pkg, .app, App Store), System Settings, Time Machine, FileVault, Keychain, Spotlight, Mission Control, Terminal, Disk Utility, Force Quit","Linux: file and permission commands (ls, cp, mv, rm, chmod, chown, sudo, su), package managers (apt, dnf), ip, df, top, ps, grep, find, man, key files (/etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, /etc/hosts, /etc/fstab, /etc/resolv.conf)","Installing applications: 32- vs 64-bit, RAM/CPU/GPU/storage requirements, distribution methods (ISO, download, image) and business impact","Cloud productivity tools: email, synced storage, collaboration suites, account setup and licensing"],"notes":["Objectives 1.1–1.11"],"labs":["Install VirtualBox (free) and build a Windows 11 evaluation VM and an Ubuntu VM; note the boot method, partition style (GPT/MBR) and file system each installer used.","On your own Windows PC, open every tool in the objectives (eventvwr.msc, diskmgmt.msc, taskschd.msc, devmgmt.msc, lusrmgr.msc, perfmon.msc, msinfo32, resmon, msconfig) and write one sentence on what each is for.","In the Ubuntu VM, create a user, a group and a shared folder, then practice `chmod`, `chown`, `sudo`, `apt install`, `df -h`, `ip addr` and `grep` until you can do them without notes."]},{"id":2,"name":"Security","w":28,"topics":["Physical security: access control vestibules, badge readers, video surveillance, alarm systems, locks, guards, bollards, fences","Logical security: least privilege, zero trust, MFA methods (authenticator apps, SMS, hardware tokens, email), SSO, MDM, DLP, IAM, directory services, access control lists","Windows security: Microsoft Defender Antivirus and Firewall, users and groups, NTFS vs share permissions, inheritance, UAC, BitLocker and BitLocker To Go, EFS, Windows Hello, run as administrator","Wireless security: WPA2 vs WPA3, AES vs TKIP, RADIUS, TACACS+, Kerberos, multifactor","Malware: virus, trojan, rootkit, ransomware, keylogger, spyware, adware, cryptominer, boot sector virus, stalkerware, fileless malware; tools such as anti-malware, recovery console, EDR/MDR/XDR, email security gateways","Social engineering and threats: phishing, vishing, smishing, QR code phishing, whaling, impersonation, tailgating, shoulder surfing, dumpster diving, evil twin, DoS/DDoS, zero-day, on-path, brute force, insider threat, SQL injection, XSS, BEC, supply chain","The seven-step malware removal procedure, in order","Workstation hardening: data-at-rest encryption, password policy, end-user best practices (screensaver locks, logging off), account management, disable AutoRun/AutoPlay, disable guest account","Mobile device security: screen locks, remote wipe, locator apps, OS updates, device encryption, remote backup, MDM, BYOD vs corporate-owned","Data destruction: shredding, drilling, degaussing, incineration; erasing/wiping vs low-level vs standard format; certificates of destruction","SOHO router hardening: default passwords, firmware updates, disabling WPS and UPnP, content filtering, port forwarding, DHCP reservations, guest networks","Browser security: trusted sources and hash checks, extensions, password managers, certificates, pop-up blockers, clearing cache, private browsing, profile sync"],"notes":["Objectives 2.1–2.11"],"labs":["On a Windows VM, create a local standard user and a folder shared over the network; set share permission Full Control and NTFS permission Read, then prove from another account that the most restrictive combination wins.","Harden your own home router: change the admin password, update firmware, disable WPS and UPnP, switch to WPA3 or WPA2-AES and create a guest network; record each setting you changed.","Download a free tool (for example 7-Zip), compute its SHA-256 with `certutil -hashfile <file> SHA256` or `Get-FileHash`, and compare it to the hash on the publisher's site before installing."]},{"id":3,"name":"Software troubleshooting","w":23,"topics":["Windows symptoms: blue screen (BSOD), degraded performance, boot problems, frequent shutdowns, services not starting, application crashes, low memory warnings, USB controller resource warnings, system instability, no OS found, slow profile load, time drift","Windows fixes: reboot, restart services, uninstall/reinstall/update apps, add resources, verify requirements, sfc and DISM, repair Windows, System Restore, reimage, roll back updates, rebuild the user profile","Using Event Viewer, Reliability Monitor, Task Manager and Safe Mode / Windows Recovery Environment to find root causes","Mobile OS and app issues: app fails to launch, close or update; slow response; poor battery life; random reboots; Bluetooth, Wi-Fi and NFC connectivity; screen won't autorotate","Mobile security issues: unofficial app stores, jailbreaking and rooting, sideloaded APKs, high network traffic, data-limit alerts, sluggish response, fake security warnings, unexpected app behavior, leaked personal files","PC security issues: unable to reach the network, fake antivirus alerts, altered or missing system files, unwanted OS notifications, failed OS updates","Browser security issues: random pop-ups, certificate warnings, redirection, degraded browser performance","Checking and repairing startup items, scheduled tasks, browser extensions and proxy settings after an infection"],"notes":["Objectives 3.1–3.4"],"labs":["Open Event Viewer and Reliability Monitor on your own PC, find the last three errors or crashes, and write down the source, event ID and a likely fix for each.","In a Windows VM, run `sfc /scannow` and `DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth`, create a restore point, install a small app, then use System Restore to roll back.","Boot the Windows VM into Safe Mode and into the Windows Recovery Environment (Shift + Restart) and list every repair option you find there."]},{"id":4,"name":"Operational procedures","w":21,"topics":["Ticketing systems: user and device information, descriptions, categories, severity, escalation levels, clear progress notes and resolutions","Asset management: inventory lists, CMDB, asset tags and IDs, procurement life cycle, warranty and licensing, assigned users","Documentation types: acceptable use policy, incident reports, SOPs, onboarding and offboarding checklists, SLAs, knowledge base articles","Change management: request forms, purpose and scope, risk analysis, change advisory board approval, sandbox testing, rollback and backup plans, end-user acceptance","Backup and recovery: full, incremental, differential and synthetic backups; testing restores; on-site vs off-site; 3-2-1 rule; grandfather-father-son rotation","Safety: ESD straps and mats, grounding, power handling, lifting technique, electrical fire safety, PPE","Environment: safety data sheets, battery and toner disposal, temperature and humidity, ventilation, UPS and surge suppressors","Prohibited content and privacy: incident response and chain of custody, licensing (EULA, DRM, open source vs commercial, personal vs corporate), PII, PCI DSS, GDPR, PHI, data retention","Professionalism: punctuality, active listening, avoiding jargon, handling difficult customers, confidentiality, setting expectations and following up","Scripting basics: .bat, .ps1, .vbs, .sh, .js, .py; use cases (automation, restarts, drive mapping, installs, backups, updates) and risks","Remote access: RDP, VPN, VNC, SSH, RMM, SPICE, WinRM, screen-sharing and file-transfer tools, and their security considerations","Artificial intelligence basics: app integration, appropriate-use policy and plagiarism, bias, hallucinations and accuracy, public vs private models and data privacy"],"notes":["Objectives 4.1–4.10"],"labs":["Write a PowerShell script that shows the computer name, OS version, free disk space and IP address (`Get-ComputerInfo`, `Get-PSDrive`, `Get-NetIPAddress`), then write the same as a Bash script in your Linux VM.","Set up a backup of a test folder with File History (Windows) or Time Machine (macOS), delete a file, restore it, and write a one-page backup SOP with a 3-2-1 plan.","Create a free ticketing board (a spreadsheet works) and log three mock tickets with category, severity, steps taken, escalation and a clear resolution note written for the user."]}],"study":{"1":[["Which Windows edition features matter for a business, and which editions have them?","Joining a domain, Group Policy Editor, BitLocker and hosting Remote Desktop need Pro, Enterprise or Education. Home has only device encryption on supported hardware and can only be a Remote Desktop client."],["Compare a clean install, an in-place upgrade and an image deployment.","A clean install wipes the drive and starts fresh. An in-place upgrade keeps apps, settings and files. Image deployment copies a prepared standard image to many PCs, often over the network with PXE."],["When would you choose GPT over MBR?","Use GPT for UEFI systems, drives over 2 TB, or when you need more than four primary partitions. MBR is for legacy BIOS systems and is limited to 2 TB and four primary partitions."],["Which file system would you use for a USB drive shared between Windows and macOS that holds 8 GB video files?","exFAT: both OSs read and write it and it has no 4 GB file size limit. FAT32 cannot store files over 4 GB, and NTFS is read-only on macOS by default."],["What do sfc, DISM and chkdsk each repair?","sfc /scannow repairs protected Windows system files. DISM /RestoreHealth repairs the component store that sfc uses as its source. chkdsk /f (or /r) checks and fixes file system errors and bad sectors on a volume."],["Name the macOS equivalents of BitLocker, File History and Task Manager's End task.","FileVault encrypts the disk, Time Machine backs up files, and Force Quit (Command+Option+Esc) or Activity Monitor ends a hung app."]],"2":[["List the seven malware removal steps in order.","1 Investigate and verify symptoms. 2 Quarantine the system. 3 Disable System Restore (Windows). 4 Remediate: update anti-malware, then scan and remove. 5 Schedule scans and run updates. 6 Enable System Restore and create a restore point. 7 Educate the end user."],["How do share and NTFS permissions combine over the network?","Windows works out the effective share permission and the effective NTFS permission separately, then applies the more restrictive of the two. Locally, only NTFS applies."],["Why is WPA3 better than WPA2-Personal?","WPA3-Personal uses SAE instead of a pre-shared key handshake, which resists offline dictionary attacks and adds forward secrecy. WPA2 with AES is still acceptable; TKIP and WEP are not."],["Which data destruction methods fit an SSD, and which don't?","Physical shredding, drilling or incineration, or the drive's secure erase / crypto-erase command. Degaussing does not work on SSDs because they store data in flash, not magnetically."],["Give five steps to harden a new SOHO router.","Change the default admin password, update firmware, use WPA3 or WPA2-AES with a strong passphrase, disable WPS and UPnP, turn off remote administration, and put visitors on a guest network."],["Explain the difference between phishing, vishing, smishing and whaling.","Phishing is fraudulent email; vishing is by voice call; smishing is by SMS text; whaling is phishing aimed at senior executives."]],"3":[["A PC shows a BSOD after a new driver. What do you try, in order?","Note the stop code, boot to Safe Mode or WinRE, roll back or uninstall the driver in Device Manager, use System Restore if needed, then check for an updated, signed driver."],["Why might a user profile load slowly, and how do you fix it?","Large roaming profiles, network drive mappings to unreachable shares, corrupt profile data or many startup apps. Trim startup items, fix mappings, and rebuild the profile if it is corrupt."],["What are the warning signs of a compromised phone?","Sluggish response, high data use or network traffic, fast battery drain, unexpected apps or behavior, fake security warnings, and personal files appearing online. Sideloading and jailbreaking raise the risk."],["A browser keeps redirecting searches to another site. What do you check?","Remove unknown extensions, reset the search engine and home page, check proxy settings and the hosts file, clear cache, and scan for malware."],["What does time drift on a PC break, and how do you fix it?","Kerberos logons, certificates and log correlation can fail. Make sure the Windows Time service is running and syncing to a valid NTP source (w32tm /resync), and replace a failing CMOS battery."]],"4":[["Compare full, incremental and differential backups for restore speed.","Full is slowest to back up but fastest to restore. Incremental backs up changes since the last backup of any type, so a restore needs the full plus every incremental. Differential backs up changes since the last full, so a restore needs the full plus the latest differential."],["What goes into a change request?","Purpose, scope, date and time, affected systems, risk analysis, responsible staff, test results from a sandbox, a rollback plan and approval from the change board."],["You find illegal content on a user's PC during a repair. What do you do?","Stop, do not alter the evidence, secure the device, report to management (and law enforcement if policy says so), document everything, and keep chain of custody."],["Name three risks of running scripts and how to reduce them.","Introducing malware, changing system settings by mistake, and crashes from resource leaks. Use trusted, reviewed scripts, test in a sandbox first and run with least privilege."],["What should a company's AI use policy cover?","Which tools are approved, what data may be entered (no confidential data in public models), checking output for hallucinations and bias, and rules against plagiarism."],["Which remote access tools would you choose for a Windows desktop, a Linux server and a fleet of PCs?","RDP for a Windows desktop, SSH for a Linux server, and an RMM platform for fleet monitoring and patching. Protect all of them with a VPN or MFA and never expose them directly to the internet."]]},"qCount":150,"hasLessons":true,"hasLessonsEs":true,"hasPbqs":true,"hasHandson":true,"hasQuestionsEs":true,"hasLevels":true});
